Date: Thu, 17 Jun 93 11:28:53 -0700
From: freeman@MasPar.COM (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: SOC/CHAT: Wolf behavior

Stanton McCandlish and I have exchanged:
 
> > (Coyotes probably aren't wolves but they are closely related. (Actually,
> > some biologists will tell you that they are the same species [...]
 
> Then they'd need to hit the books again.  Coyotes are quite different from
> wolves. [...]
 
The issue is by no means firmly decided, and I am not qualified to have
an opinion; however, I summarize the "same-species" argument as follows:
 
There is a classic biological test for whether two groups of organisms
(call them "A" and "B") constitute one species or more than one.  It runs
as follows:
 
*If* the ranges of the two groups in nature overlap (and if they don't,
you can't use this test), and *if* members of A and B mate regularly,
and *if* they produce offspring, and *if* those offspring can in turn
mate and produce offspring both with other such offspring and with members
of both A and B, *then* you have not two species but one.
 
The rationale for this argument is that under the conditions described,
genes from A and B are going to get thoroughly mixed up eventually, so
that there will eventually be a continuum all the way from A to B, even
if there isn't one at the time you are asking the question.  (That time
might for example be after two populations of what were originally the
same species, that have been isolated for a while by geological processes,
have been brought back into contact by more geological processes.  Such
events probably happened a lot in the ice ages, a major icecap being a
formidable temporary barrier for some kinds of critters.)

In the context of wolf/coyote, all the conditions described are met to
some degree -- the so-called "red wolf" seems well-established as a
wolf/coyote hybrid, so the argument that the two animals are merely
variant populations of one species follows.
 
There are some problems with this argument:  The conditions are
decribed as if they were straight booleans, which they aren't.  How
much overlap of range are you talking about?  How often do A/B matings
occur in proportion to A/A and B/B?  How reproductively successful is
A/B mating?  And so on.
 
"Species" is one of those words that is most interesting when you are
least sure of what it means.  It may be that wolves and coyotes are in
fact caught in the act of speciating -- becoming two different species.
 
The test I have been talking about is sometimes expressed in more succinct
form as "members of the same species are reproductively successful (that
is, they can have offspring and their offspring can have offspring)".  In
the same vein, a criterion often used for classifying species into genera
is that members of different species within the same genus can have
offspring but those offspring cannot themselves have further offspring.
Slippery booleans also lead to uncertainties with this one.
 
It is also interesting to think about wolf/dog and coyote/dog in the
context of "how many species".
                                    -- Jay Freeman
