Now, I'm a great fan of Lakoff's work, I use MWLB and especially WFDT
extensively in my classes, and assure my students that this is True
Semantics, and to avoid that "objectivist" bullshit at all costs.  And
Lakoff and I are allies in the "Cognitive Linguistics" war against the
Empire of Chomsky and his evil minions.  And so I got that letter Joan
is talking about, of which Richard Kenneway says:

> > I saw Lakoff's letter, and it seemed to me like a piece of
> > pseudo-scientific gobbledygook, starting from his desired conclusion and
> > spinning a just-so story to support it.  (I don't have the letter, and I
> > can't remember where I saw it, so I can't be more specific, but I might try
> > if you send it to me.)

And sad to say, I'm 100% with Richard on this.  The letter was embarassing
garbage.  I also don't have a copy (it was a source of cognitive/emotional
distress to have it in the same room with my copy of WFDT), so I can't go
into detail either, but I can confidently assure folks that it would not
play well on this list.
      And Eric Raymond, in Lakoff's defense, says:

> There's a hallowed tradition by which linguists turn into idiots when they
> do politics.  Consider Chomsky as a better-known example, and don't assume
> that Lakoff is bogus in his own field.

I'd call this a bit hasty.  Lakoff's letter claimed quite explicitly to be
an application of the ideas set forth in MWLB and WFDT.  When a theoretician
presents an application of his ideas, and the application is garbage, there's
a clear implication with respect to the theories.  In this case I think
the inference would be wrong, but it's not unreasonable.
     And then there's Chomsky. The cases are not parallel in a couple of 
respects, most importantly that, IMO (nothing remotely humble here) Chomsky
*is* bogus in his own field.  His linguistic and his political writing show
the same intellectual dishonesty and thuggery, working deductively
from undemonstrable premises, sneaking in weak assumptions and then
treating them as proven, and ignoring, distorting, or airily dismissing 
opposing arguments or awkward evidence.  His prominence in linguistics
is an unhappy commentary on the field, but no reason to consider his
political idiocies anomalous.


