From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: TECH: Books added to my nanotech library & recent Science articles

I've found a few more interesting books and articles that fit in 
quite nicely with my theoretical nanotech collection.  

_Creating Artificial Life: Self-Organization_ by Edward Rietman,
1993 Windcrest Books.
This has chapters on the math of a-life (ranging from set and
graph theory to strange attractors), morphogenesis, genetic
engineering & the origin of life, computer viruses, and cellular
automaton.  There are some programs (in C, Pascal and BASIC)
which are very crude simulations of molecular species competition,
Turing morphogenesis, simple differential equations solver
and plotter, etc.  This book is _very_ technical, completely
the opposite of the puffball _Nanotech Playhouse_.  If you
interested in how to design a self-replicating factory, this book and
the A-Life conference proceedings, especially the stuff on
hypercycles/autocatalytic sets, is the place to start.

_Assembly Automation, A Management Handbook_, Frank J. Riley, 1983.
Despite the name, this is the only book on assembly I've seen that
is rich in engineering detail, and concentrates on real assembly
problems and the wide variety of assembly machines being used
in industry, instead of theoretical/academic models of robotics.  The 
book covers parts transfer and feeding, integration of fabrication and
packaging with assembly, joining and fastening, control, etc.
The book is also unique in emphasizing mechanical control (eg cams)
over electronic control.  It thus fits in well with Drexler's 
emphasis on mechanical computation.  Some of this stuff is
surprisingly reminiscent of chemical operations (eg the vibratory parts
feeder and its use of orientation and "handles" on the parts is
covered in detail).

Science, 260:753, "Desinger Solids" covers work being done by 
chemists to develop "Meccano", "Tinkertoy" etc. 
molecular building-block sets that Brownian-assemble into
complex, "programmable" structures.  One of the projects
involves DNA that assembles into squares, cubes, lattices,
etc.  

Other recent Science articles have covered:
* large monocyclic carbon rings about 5 times the diameter
of buckyballs (created in the process of making fullerenes).
* optical "gas crystals", formed by atoms trapped in regular
laser beam interference patterns.  1D and 3D structures can
be formed, and they hope to use the edge of the 3D structure
