Subject: SOC/CHAT: Wolf behavior

Quoth Perry E. Metzger, verily I saith unto thee:

> Domestic dogs can interbreed freely with wolves, and produce fertile
> offspring, which is the usual definition of "species". 

That is a rather old and limiting definition.  I had heard this was
suspect.  Ever heard of a lyger?  Asiatic wildcats are a separate species
(as far as we can tell) from Felis domesticus, yet they can be successfully
bred.  If I am wrong, and this definition does still hold without serious
challenge, I stand to be corrected.

> Indeed,
> domestic dogs now appear, from genetic studies, to be nothing more
> than domesticated wolves that have been selectively bred for five
> thousand years.

Hate to ask for a ref, since I cannot provide one either for the above (it
comes from a high-school biology lecture, in which it was stated that this
definition holds often enough to be USED generally, but it was recognized
that it is not truly valid.  Seems analogous to the use of old Newtonian
physics in many fields, because it's accurate enough for the purpose most
of the time.  I'd also remind you that there really may be no such thing
as a species; "species" is just a human categorization imposed on what we
observe.  Last I heard, dogs were a mix of wolves and other things,
more or less "dogs", that were NOT wolves. Native Mexicans also had some
sort of dog, long long before contact with anyone from the E. hemisphere
(unless you buy the Prince Llewellyn's Voyage idea!), and it seems HIGHLY 
unlikely that 2 isolated populations would selectively breed wolves into
very similar dogs.  Seems far more reasonable that dogs already existed,
and the dogs we have now are a mix of that and wolves.  [Anyone know
anything about dingos?  As far as I know these were not bred by people,
but evolved the way they are "naturally", though by now escaped domestic
dogs have likely mingled with them].  I cannot prove this from home.  If
anyone thinks it is important, I suppose I could go to the library, but I
personally don't think this is worth the trouble.
 
> Whether coyotes are more different or less different is not a matter I
> have personal knowledge of, but certainly saying "no more related to
> wolves than are domestic dogs" is like saying "no more related to
> Africans than are Amerinds".

If I am wrong on the dogs part, then yep, you got me.  But that just makes
it a bad example.  I still stand by my core statement: coyote != wolf. 

In fairness, I should also add that one would have to give strict
definitions of "wolf" and "coyote" for the issue to even be meaningful,
so perhaps this is another thread that should be retired?  Your call.

